.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt RACH2-RECOVER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm rach2-recover
.Nd recovery menu for a machine that will not boot
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
command lists disks, checks a file system, restores a saved disk image,
installs a kernel, or starts a shell.
It takes no options.
It runs from the recovery system, which mounts nothing from the disk
being repaired.
A root that cannot be mounted can therefore still be overwritten.
Each operation can be cancelled before it writes.
The menu reports results and refusals with the prefix
.Ql rach2-recover: .
.Pp
The menu entries are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 1: what this machine has
List disks and their partitions from
.Pa /proc/disk .
Each disk is reported in this form:
.Dl rach2-recover: /dev/diskN: MODEL, serial S, BYTES bytes, TRANSPORT, table T
Its partition records follow.
.It 2: check a file system
Ask for a partition, for example
.Pa /dev/disk0p3 .
An empty answer returns to the menu.
A nonexistent path is reported as
.Ql DEVICE is not a device of this machine .
Run
.Ql e2fsck -f -n
without writing to the volume.
Report a clean file system on success.
Otherwise report faults and show
.Ql e2fsck -f -p DEVICE
as the repair command.
The menu does not run that repair.
.It 3: put a saved image back on a disk
Ask for a whole disk, such as
.Pa /dev/disk0 .
The node must be a disk listed by this machine.
A partition is refused because the image begins with a partition table.
An empty answer returns to the menu.
.Pp
Look for the install stick by mounting removable exFAT partitions under
.Pa /mnt/recover
and checking for
.Pa rach2-install.manifest .
When found, offer the image named by that manifest.
Otherwise ask for an image with a hash in
.Pa IMAGE.sha256 .
An empty answer selects the offered image, or returns to the menu if
none was offered.
.Pp
The image must be a file.
Its expected hash is read from
.Pa IMAGE.sha256
or, if that file is absent, from a manifest beside the image that names it.
An absent hash or an unreadable image length stops the operation.
Display the image length and disk identity, then require the disk's node
name to be typed back.
Any other answer cancels without writing.
.Pp
Read and hash the image before writing.
A mismatch with the expected hash stops the operation.
Write the image at offset 0 with
.Ql reflash -p ,
then read it back with
.Ql reflash --verify .
A write failure or differing readback hash is reported.
For an image with a GUID table, run
.Ql diskctl relocate ,
which moves the table's backup from where the image ends to the disk's
last sectors and reloads the partition table; for an image with an MBR,
run
.Ql diskctl reread .
If either cannot, report that the image was written and verified and that
the machine must be rebooted before anything on that disk is mounted.
On success, report that the disk was restored and read back.
.It 4: install a kernel on a boot partition
Ask for a boot partition, for example
.Pa /dev/disk0p1 ,
then a kernel file.
An empty answer returns to the menu.
A nonexistent device path or a kernel that is not a file is refused.
Run
.Ql bootctl --target PARTITION kernel FILE .
Report whether the kernel was installed as the candidate and can be
booted once to try it.
.It 5: a shell
Start a shell.
Use
.Ql exit
to return to the menu.
.It 6: leave
Leave the menu.
.El
.Pp
An interrupt, termination, or hangup signal ends the run and unmounts
the volume the menu mounted under
.Pa /mnt/recover .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /mnt/recover
.It Pa /proc/disk
Disk identities and partition records.
.It Pa /mnt/recover
Mount point used to find the install image.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr recovery 7 ,
.Xr bootctl 8 ,
.Xr e2fsck 8 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8 ,
.Xr reflash 8
